Make a host-requested bypass authoritative in the graph.

Stock JUCE hands bypass to the plugin whenever the plugin exports its own
bypass parameter (VST3 kIsBypass): Node::setBypassed() sets that parameter and
the render sequence keeps calling processBlock(), trusting the plugin to pass
audio through itself. A plugin whose exported parameter is dead then never
bypasses at all - Uhhyou's OrdinaryPhaser 0.1.7 exports one and ignores it,
so the rack's bypass toggle audibly did nothing. Nor can the host fall back to
processBlockBypassed(): the hosted-VST3 override routes that straight back
into the same parameter (updateBypass in juce_VST3PluginFormatImpl.h).

So when the *host* asked for bypass, enforce it in the graph: skip the
plugin's processing entirely and pass the buffer through. The node's buffer
already holds its input in place, so passing through is clearing whatever
output channels the input does not cover - exactly what the AudioProcessor
base implementation of processBlockBypassed does, minus its zero-latency
assertion (the graph performs no latency compensation of its own). A plugin
bypassing *itself* through its own parameter - from its own editor - is left
alone, as before. Node::setBypassed still writes the plugin's parameter too,
which keeps a working plugin's editor UI in step; it just no longer decides
what the graph renders.

diff --git a/modules/juce_audio_processors_headless/processors/juce_AudioProcessorGraph.cpp b/modules/juce_audio_processors_headless/processors/juce_AudioProcessorGraph.cpp
--- a/modules/juce_audio_processors_headless/processors/juce_AudioProcessorGraph.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_audio_processors_headless/processors/juce_AudioProcessorGraph.cpp
@@ -887,10 +887,17 @@
             if (processor.isSuspended())
             {
                 buffer.clear();
             }
             else
             {
-                const auto bypass = node->isBypassed() && processor.getBypassParameter() == nullptr;
+                /*  PLECTRIFY_HOST_OWNED_BYPASS: a bypass the host requested is the
+                    host's to enforce. Delegating it to the plugin's exported
+                    bypass parameter leaves a plugin whose parameter is dead
+                    audibly running forever. A plugin bypassing itself through
+                    its own parameter is untouched by this.
+                */
+                const auto bypass = node->userRequestedBypass()
+                                    || (node->isBypassed() && processor.getBypassParameter() == nullptr);
                 processWithBuffer (c.globalIO, bypass, buffer, *midiBuffer);
             }
         }
@@ -961,8 +968,21 @@
         template <typename Value>
         static void processImpl (bool bypass, AudioProcessor& p, AudioBuffer<Value>& audio, MidiBuffer& midi)
         {
             if (bypass)
-                p.processBlockBypassed (audio, midi);
+            {
+                /*  PLECTRIFY_HOST_OWNED_BYPASS: never dispatch to the plugin's
+                    processBlockBypassed - the hosted-VST3 override routes it
+                    back into the same exported bypass parameter this patch
+                    exists to stop trusting. The buffer already holds the node's
+                    input in place, so passing through is clearing whatever
+                    output channels the input does not cover - what the
+                    AudioProcessor base implementation does, minus its
+                    zero-latency assertion (the graph does no latency
+                    compensation of its own).
+                */
+                for (int ch = p.getMainBusNumInputChannels(); ch < p.getTotalNumOutputChannels(); ++ch)
+                    audio.clear (ch, 0, audio.getNumSamples());
+            }
             else
                 p.processBlock (audio, midi);
         }
